From 63c1644799cbba38e771436090de347dd546fa70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 22:29:24 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] HandleBRK: leave the break alone when brk is asked to go
 below its start

Linux refuses a break below the start of the data segment by returning the
current break with nothing changed (mm/mmap.c brk). HandleBRK instead set
the break size to 0 and unmapped the whole break area. glibc's malloc heap
lives there, so the next malloc'd access faults.

Trigger: brk() with an address below the initial break, e.g. brk(4096).

Evidence, 64K host, FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force, a static x86-64 test that calls
syscall(SYS_brk, 4096), then mallocs and writes 100 KB:
  before: SIGSEGV (core dumped)
  after:  brk-below-start: unchanged=yes heap-usable=yes
Linux (the same test on an aarch64 Raspberry Pi 5): unchanged=yes, heap
usable.

Found in POWERarm by the A64Syscalls sys_mem test.
---
 .../Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/Syscalls.cpp   | 11 +++--------
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/Syscalls.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/Syscalls.cpp
index c60a3846c..f23de317e 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/Syscalls.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/Syscalls.cpp
@@ -1094,14 +1094,9 @@ uint64_t SyscallHandler::HandleBRK(FEXCore::Core::CpuStateFrame* Frame, void* Ad
     // Allocating out data space
     uint64_t NewEnd = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(Addr);
     if (NewEnd < DataSpace) {
-      // Not allowed to move brk end below original start
-      // Set the size to zero
-      DataSpaceSize = 0;
-
-      // Munmap the whole space.
-      [[maybe_unused]] auto ok = GuestMunmap(Frame->Thread, reinterpret_cast<void*>(DataSpace), DataSpaceMappedSize);
-      LOGMAN_THROW_A_FMT(ok != -1, "Munmap failed");
-      DataSpaceMappedSize = 0;
+      // mm/mmap.c brk: a break below the start of the data segment is refused
+      // and the current break is returned, with nothing unmapped. This used to
+      // unmap the whole break area, taking a live malloc heap with it.
     } else {
       uint64_t NewSize = NewEnd - DataSpace;
       // HOST: DataSpaceMappedSize describes real mappings, so the emulated break
